In Configure.help you can read:

CONFIG_UFS_FS
  BSD and derivate versions of Unix (such as SunOS, FreeBSD, NetBSD,
  OpenBSD and NeXTstep) use a file system called UFS. Some System V
  Unixes can create and mount hard disk partitions and diskettes using
  this file system as well. .....

There is quite a bit more but you can find that yourself.

> I don't understand why it's trying to use reiserfs when mounting root.

Unless you will tell explicitely otherwise with -t, or in /etc/fstab
entries, then 'mount' is going through a list of possibilities and is
trying each in turn until something will fit.  This usually works ok but
sometimes things are getting confused; especially in a presence of some
"random garbage" in initial disk sectors if a disk was used previously.

   dd if=/dev/zero count=8 > "your_disk_device_here"

from a shell on an installation ramdisk and before you started to
install anything usually resolves the matter.  This also helps to
clear "wrong type of a partition table" problems.

  Michal


